  "id": "Compound::DB00338 - Gene::1562",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes, including Cyp2c18, through a shared mechanism of participation in the arachidonic acid epoxygenase activity. This pathway is supported by three distinct but overlapping evidence chains, each linking Omeprazole to Cyp2c18 via a common intermediate, Cyp2c8 or Cyp2c19, which in turn participates in the same enzymatic activity. The binding of Omeprazole to these CYP enzymes suggests a potential role in modulating the metabolism of arachidonic acid, which is a key precursor in the production of pro-inflammatory mediators. The consistent presence of this pathway across different CYP isoforms indicates a general mechanism of action that may be relevant to the pharmacological effects of Omeprazole. The integration of these pathways highlights a mechanistic link between Omeprazole and Cyp2c18, emphasizing the importance of CYP enzymes in the metabolic processing of arachidonic acid and its downstream effects.",
